---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Building Crypt Shift: Rules Before Graphics';
const description =
  'How a dormant Phaser experiment became Crypt Shift: a turn-based dungeon game with deterministic daily maps and tested game rules.';
---

<BaseLayout title={`${title} | Eddie's Games`} description={description}>
  <article class='article'>
    <header class='article-header'>
      <p class='eyebrow'>Behind the Games</p>

      <h1>{title}</h1>

      <p class='dek'>
        Crypt Shift started with coloured shapes, a small dungeon and one
        important rule: make the game work before worrying about how pretty the
        monsters are.
      </p>

      <p class='meta'>September 19, 2026 · Development Journal</p>
    </header>

    <div class='article-body'>
      <p>
        The next game for Eddies Games needed to be more graphical.
      </p>

      <p>
        Most of the existing games are puzzles built around grids, letters,
        numbers or matching symbols. They work well in a browser, but I wanted
        the next project to feel more like a traditional game: a visible player,
        enemies, movement, objectives and an actual dungeon to explore.
      </p>

      <p>
        The result is <strong>Crypt Shift</strong>, a small turn-based dungeon
        game built with Phaser.
      </p>

      <p>
        It currently uses simple placeholder shapes instead of finished artwork.
        That is intentional. Before spending time drawing heroes, guardians,
        runes and stone walls, I wanted to know whether moving around the crypt
        was actually enjoyable.
      </p>

      <h2>Starting With an Existing Experiment</h2>

      <p>
        Crypt Shift did not begin completely from scratch. The site already had
        a dormant Phaser experiment called Dungeon Dash. It proved that Phaser
        could run inside the existing Astro and Cloudflare site without needing
        a separate application or deployment system.
      </p>

      <p>
        Dungeon Dash used real-time movement and enemy chasing. It was useful as
        a technical experiment, but Crypt Shift needed a more deliberate pace.
        Instead of constantly moving, the guardians now wait for the player to
        make a move.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        <p>One player move. One enemy response.</p>
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        That change turned the game into something closer to a tactical puzzle.
        The player can stop, inspect the room and decide where to move next.
        Nothing happens until a valid move is made.
      </p>

      <p>
        Reusing the earlier Phaser work meant the new game could remain part of
        the existing Eddies Games structure. Crypt Shift lives alongside the
        other browser games rather than becoming another repository, build
        system or mysterious folder waiting to cause trouble six months from
        now.
      </p>

      <h2>The First Playable Room</h2>

      <p>
        The initial version uses a twelve-by-ten tile grid. Each tile is
        fifty-six pixels square, giving enough room for readable characters
        without making the game enormous on a laptop screen.
      </p>

      <p>The current rules are straightforward:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Collect all three runes.</li>
        <li>Avoid or outmanoeuvre the crypt guardians.</li>
        <li>Reach the exit after collecting the runes.</li>
        <li>Survive with three health points.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The player can move with the arrow keys or WASD. On a touch screen, the
        player can tap an adjacent tile. This gives the game a usable mobile
        control method without covering half the dungeon with virtual buttons.
      </p>

      <p>
        The first canvas was technically responsive but still too large for a
        MacBook screen. Responsive does not automatically mean sensible. The
        game container now limits both its width and height, allowing Phaser's
        canvas to scale down while preserving the shape of the dungeon.
      </p>

      <p>
        That small adjustment made the prototype considerably easier to test and
        also established an important constraint for the final artwork: the
        graphics must remain readable when the game is scaled down.
      </p>

      <h2>Rules Outside the Renderer</h2>

      <p>
        Phaser handles the visible game: drawing the room, accepting input and
        updating the display. The actual rules live in a separate JavaScript
        module.
      </p>

      <p>
        This separation means the rules do not need a browser canvas to be
        tested. Movement, collisions, rune collection, enemy behaviour, damage
        and winning the room can all be checked without launching Phaser.
      </p>

      <p>
        It also prevents the rendering code from becoming the authority on what
        is happening. Phaser displays the game state, but it does not secretly
        invent a second set of rules while nobody is looking.
      </p>

      <p>
        The project currently has thirteen automated tests covering the core
        game rules and daily map generation. The test suite runs with Vitest as
        part of the existing site project.
      </p>

      <h2>A Different Crypt Every Day</h2>

      <p>
        The first room was fixed, which was useful while developing the basic
        movement and combat. The next step was a daily dungeon.
      </p>

      <p>
        Crypt Shift now generates a map from the current Eastern Time date. The
        same date always produces the same room, so every player receives the
        same daily challenge.
      </p>

      <p>
        A seeded random-number generator builds the maze, opens some additional
        passages and then places the exit, runes and guardians. The exit is
        positioned at the furthest reachable floor tile from the player's
        starting position.
      </p>

      <p>
        Random generation creates one fairly obvious problem: random maps can be
        complete rubbish.
      </p>

      <p>
        A generator can easily place an objective behind an unreachable wall,
        forget a rune or create a room that technically exists but cannot be
        completed. That is where automated testing becomes particularly useful.
      </p>

      <p>
        The test suite generates one thousand seeded rooms and checks every one
        of them. Each room must contain a valid player position, an exit, exactly
        three runes and at least one guardian. Every required objective must also
        be reachable.
      </p>

      <p>
        Testing one thousand maps does not mathematically prove that every map
        forever will be perfect, but it catches considerably more trouble than
        manually playing Tuesday's dungeon and declaring the generator done.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the Graphics Are Still Shapes</h2>

      <p>
        The current player is a blue circle. Guardians are red diamonds, runes
        are purple stars and the exit is a green rectangle.
      </p>

      <p>
        Nobody is likely to frame a screenshot.
      </p>

      <p>
        Those placeholders make development faster because every object is
        immediately recognisable. More importantly, they allow the size and
        readability of the game to be tested before artwork begins.
      </p>

      <p>
        The planned sprite frames are fifty-six pixels square to match the tile
        grid. The visible artwork will sit inside those frames with enough space
        around it to avoid characters colliding visually with walls and
        neighbouring tiles.
      </p>

      <p>
        Once the final visual style is introduced, the rules underneath should
        not need to change. A guardian can become an animated skeleton, stone
        creature or suspiciously angry haunted wardrobe without rewriting how
        it moves.
      </p>

      <h2>What Comes Next</h2>

      <p>
        The daily dungeon gives Crypt Shift a replayable mode, but generated
        rooms are only part of the plan. The next major addition will be a set of
        handcrafted levels.
      </p>

      <p>
        Regular levels can introduce mechanics in a controlled order and create
        more deliberate puzzles than a generator. They will use the same compact
        text-based map format as the daily rooms, allowing each level to be
        validated automatically before it reaches the game.
      </p>

      <p>
        After that comes the graphical work: proper dungeon tiles, character
        sprites, animation, effects and sound. Mobile play will continue to be
        tested as those assets arrive, because detailed artwork is not much use
        if it becomes an attractive blur on a phone.
      </p>

      <p>
        Crypt Shift is still an early playable build, but the foundation is now
        doing what it should. The rules are separated from the display, the
        daily maps are repeatable, generated rooms are tested and the game fits
        on an actual screen.
      </p>

      <p>
        Now it is ready to stop looking like an exceptionally organised
        collection of coloured geometry.
      </p>
    </div>

    <footer class='article-footer'>
      <a href='/articles/'>← All articles</a>
      <a href='/play/crypt-shift'>Play Crypt Shift →</a>
    </footer>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .article {
    width: min(100%, 820px);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 56px 0 72px;
  }

  .article-header {
    padding-bottom: 32px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 12px;
    color: var(--ember);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .article-header h1 {
    max-width: 760px;
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(2.25rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
    line-height: 0.98;
    letter-spacing: -0.045em;
  }

  .dek {
    max-width: 700px;
    margin: 24px 0 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: clamp(1.05rem, 2vw, 1.3rem);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .meta {
    margin: 20px 0 0;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .article-body {
    padding-top: 22px;
    font-size: 1.08rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .article-body p {
    margin: 1.25em 0;
  }

  .article-body h2 {
    margin: 2.4em 0 0.7em;
    font-size: clamp(1.55rem, 3vw, 2.15rem);
    line-height: 1.15;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
  }

  .article-body ul {
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
    padding-left: 1.4rem;
  }

  .article-body li {
    margin: 0.5rem 0;
  }

  blockquote {
    margin: 2rem 0;
    padding: 6px 0 6px 22px;
    border-left: 4px solid var(--ember);
  }

  blockquote p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 3vw, 1.65rem);
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .article-footer {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 24px;
    margin-top: 48px;
    padding-top: 24px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .article-footer a {
    color: var(--ember);
    font-weight: 800;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .article-footer a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .article {
      padding: 36px 0 52px;
    }

    .article-footer {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 12px;
    }
  }
</style>
